export const MR6={title:'Planning and running partnership activities',hook:'A great idea becomes a great activity only with planning: who does what, when, with which supplies and under whose supervision. MRC units plan with the same tools used in real emergency operations—clear objectives, assigned roles and a debrief afterward. Using these tools makes your activities safer, smoother and more impressive to judges.',objectives:['Write goals and SMART objectives for an activity.','Assign roles and responsibilities with the MRC.','Plan logistics, safety and permissions.','Conduct an after-action review to improve future activities.'],sections:[
['Goals and SMART objectives',[
'Start each activity with a **goal**—the broad change you want—and **SMART objectives**: **S**pecific, **M**easurable, **A**chievable, **R**elevant and **T**ime-bound. For example, goal: “Improve heat-safety knowledge among outdoor workers.” Objective: “By July 31, distribute MRC-approved bilingual heat-safety cards to 300 outdoor workers at four job sites and have at least 60 complete a three-question check.”',
'Objectives guide your planning and make results easy to report. Judges score the **accomplishment of goals and objectives**, so write them down **before** the activity and compare results afterward. If you exceeded them, say so; if you fell short, explain what you learned.',
'Agree on objectives **with your MRC unit leader**. The unit may have its own targets—such as the number of residents vaccinated at a clinic—that your chapter can help meet.',
order('Writing a SMART objective',['State who you will reach','State what will change','Make it measurable','Check it is achievable with the MRC','Link it to the community need','Set a deadline'])]],
['Roles and responsibilities',[
'Clarify **who does what**. The MRC unit typically provides **subject-matter expertise**, **training**, **supervision**, **access** to events and **approval** of materials. The HOSA chapter typically provides **volunteers**, **outreach**, **materials development** (with approval), **setup and logistics** and **documentation**. Write these roles down; the portfolio’s partnership interaction description asks for roles and responsibilities **during planning and during the activity**.',
"Confirm supervision for every activity: who from the MRC will be on site, how students will reach them and what students should do if a situation arises outside their role. Clear supervision protects students, the people you serve and the MRC.",
'Within the team, assign a **lead** for each activity, a **communications contact** for the MRC, a **documentation lead** for photos and metrics and a **materials lead**. Rotate roles across activities so every member gains experience—another way to show **impact on the HOSA chapter**.',
'MRC operations use **Incident Command System (ICS)** ideas such as a single leader, clear reporting lines and a manageable number of people per supervisor. Asking your MRC partner to explain the ICS structure at an event is a great learning opportunity to describe in your presentation.',
match('Role and typical responsibility',[['MRC unit leader','Approves materials and supervises volunteers'],['Activity lead (student)','Coordinates the team for one activity'],['Communications contact','Single point of contact with the MRC'],['Documentation lead','Photos, sign-in sheets and metrics'],['Materials lead','Prepares flyers, kits and supplies'],['Advisor','Supervision and school permissions']])]],
['Logistics and timelines',[
'Build a **timeline** backward from the event date: confirm the date and location with the MRC, request school approvals, recruit student volunteers, complete any required MRC training, prepare and approve materials, arrange transportation, confirm supplies and hold a pre-event briefing. Send reminders a week and a day before.',
'Make a **supply checklist** (sign-in sheets, name tags, flyers, tables, PPE if needed, water, first aid kit) and a **run-of-show** with times and assignments. Have a backup plan for weather, low turnout or missing volunteers.',
'Track volunteers and **hours** with a sign-in and sign-out sheet at every event. Accurate hours are needed for the Verification Form’s impact data and for the MRC Activity Reporting System.',
order('Activity planning timeline',['Confirm date and location with the MRC','Get school and parent permissions','Recruit and train student volunteers','Prepare materials and get MRC approval','Arrange transportation and supplies','Hold a pre-event briefing','Run the activity and record sign-ins','Debrief and send the activity summary'])]],
['Safety, permissions and professionalism',[
'Follow your **school’s policies** for off-campus activities: permission slips, transportation rules and adult supervision. Follow the **MRC unit’s policies** for training, identification, PPE and scope of duties. Never perform tasks beyond your training, and never self-deploy to an emergency.',
'Get **consent** before photographing community members, especially at health-related events where privacy is important; many organizations prefer photos that do not show faces of clients. Protect any personal information you see—never share names or health details.',
'Act professionally: arrive on time, wear appropriate attire or MRC/HOSA identification, put phones away while working and treat everyone with respect. You represent HOSA, your school and the MRC.',
sort('Professional practice?',['Professional','Unprofessional'],[['Wearing ID and arriving early',0],['Posting a photo of a clinic client without consent',1],['Staying with your assigned supervisor',0],['Scrolling on your phone at the registration table',1],['Keeping client information private',0],['Taking on a task you have not been trained for',1]])]],
['After-action reviews',[
'After each activity, hold a short **after-action review (AAR)**, a practice used in emergency management. Ask: **What was supposed to happen? What actually happened? What went well? What could be improved?** Include your MRC partner if possible. Write down **lessons learned** and **action items** for next time.',
'AARs improve quality across the year and give you material for the portfolio and presentation: “After our first workshop, we learned that a large-print handout was needed, so we redesigned it, and completed plans rose from 40% to 70%.” This shows growth and **impact on the HOSA chapter**.',
'Share a brief AAR summary with your unit leader along with the activity report. It shows professionalism and helps the unit plan future partnerships.',
order('After-action review questions',['What was supposed to happen?','What actually happened?','What went well?','What could be improved?','What will we do differently next time?'])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Clinic day',{a:{text:'Your chapter will help at an MRC flu clinic on Saturday. (Scenario invented for practice.) What must be ready beforehand?',choices:[['Permissions, trained volunteers, assigned roles, a sign-in sheet and approved materials','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing; just show up','b','Plan and confirm everything.',false]]},b:{text:'During the clinic, a parent asks a student whether the vaccine is safe for their child with allergies. What should the student do?',choices:[['Refer the question to the MRC nurse or clinician','c','Correct. Stay within your role.',true],['Give medical advice','c','Clinical questions go to professionals.',false]]},c:{text:'After the clinic, the team holds an after-action review and sends the unit leader a summary with hours and the number of people served.',end:true}})]],
["Working with diverse communities",["Effective preparedness reaches **everyone**. Practice **cultural humility**: learn about the communities you serve, listen more than you speak and ask community leaders what would help. Use **plain language**, offer materials in the languages spoken locally and use qualified interpreters or bilingual volunteers.","Make events **accessible**: choose locations reachable by public transit and wheelchair, offer large-print materials, provide seating and shade and schedule events at times families can attend. Ask your MRC partner about local organizations—faith groups, community centers and cultural associations—that can help you reach people.","Accessible, culturally respectful activities often have the greatest impact, and they are excellent evidence of serving vulnerable populations.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Inclusive practice?","buckets":["Inclusive","Not inclusive"],"items":[["Offering materials in Spanish and Vietnamese",0],["Holding an event only reachable by car",1],["Providing large-print handouts",0],["Using medical jargon without explanation",1],["Partnering with a community center trusted by residents",0],["Scheduling every event during work hours",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Write a goal and SMART objectives with the MRC before each activity and compare results afterward; judges score accomplishment of goals and objectives.','Define roles: the MRC provides expertise, training, supervision and approval; the chapter provides volunteers, outreach, materials, logistics and documentation; assign student leads and rotate roles; learn ICS basics.','Plan backward with a timeline, supply checklist, run-of-show and sign-in sheets for hours; follow school and MRC safety rules, get photo consent, protect privacy, act professionally and hold an after-action review after every activity.'],terms:[['SMART objective','A specific, measurable, achievable, relevant and time-bound target.'],['After-action review','A structured debrief to identify what went well and what to improve.'],['Incident Command System','A standard structure for organizing emergency and event operations.']],mcqs:[q('What does the T in SMART stand for?','Time-bound','Total','Team','Tested','Set a deadline.'),q('Who typically approves materials for a partnership activity?','The MRC unit leader','Any student','The judges','No one','Approval required.'),q('Why use sign-in and sign-out sheets?','To track volunteer hours accurately','To collect autographs','To replace permission slips','They are not needed','Impact data.'),q('Which question belongs in an after-action review?','What could be improved?','Who is to blame?','How many likes did we get?','What should we hide?','Lessons learned.'),q('If a community member asks a student a medical question, the student should:','Refer it to an MRC clinician','Give advice','Ignore the person','Guess','Stay within role.'),q('Which is a professional practice?','Keeping client information private','Posting client photos without consent','Using phones while working','Arriving late','Respect privacy.'),q('A run-of-show is:','A schedule of times and assignments for an event','A movie review','A dress code','A photo album','Logistics tool.'),q('The ICS emphasizes:','A single leader and clear reporting lines','No leaders','Everyone acting independently','Only paperwork','Organized operations.')],frq:{prompt:'Plan one partnership activity from start to finish, including SMART objectives, roles, logistics, safety and an after-action review.',rubric:[['Objectives',['goal','smart','specific','measurable','deadline','mrc']],['Roles',['mrc','approve','supervise','chapter','volunteers','lead','documentation']],['Logistics and safety',['timeline','permission','training','supplies','sign-in','consent','privacy']],['Review',['after-action','went well','improve','lessons','summary','report']]],model:'Our activity is a heat-safety outreach day for outdoor workers, planned with our MRC unit because the county reported rising heat illness. Our goal is to improve heat-safety knowledge, and our SMART objective is that by July 31 we will distribute 300 MRC-approved bilingual heat-safety cards at four job sites and have at least 60 workers complete a three-question check. The MRC unit leader will provide expertise on heat illness, approve our cards, arrange access to the job sites and assign a nurse volunteer to supervise, while our chapter designs the cards, recruits eight student volunteers, handles setup and documents the day. Within our team, Lina leads the activity, Omar is the MRC communications contact, Priya leads documentation and Sam prepares materials. Working backward from the date, we will get permission slips, complete the MRC’s volunteer orientation, finalize and approve the cards two weeks early, arrange transportation with our advisor and hold a briefing the day before. On the day, we will use a sign-in and sign-out sheet for hours, bring water and a first aid kit, wear HOSA and MRC identification, photograph only with consent and refer medical questions to the nurse. Afterward, we will hold an after-action review with the MRC, asking what was supposed to happen, what happened, what went well and what to improve, and send the unit leader a summary with hours, cards distributed and check results.'},sources:[RULES,MRC,READY_PLAN]};

export const MR7={title:'Measuring and documenting impact',hook:'“We helped a lot of people” is forgettable. “Twelve students contributed 96 hours to help the MRC vaccinate 340 residents—28% more than last year’s clinic” is not. Judges score impact on the community and on the HOSA chapter, and the portfolio asks for key metrics with every activity. Planning how to measure impact from the start makes your results credible and compelling.',objectives:['Choose output and outcome metrics for partnership activities.','Track volunteer hours and people impacted accurately.','Document activities with dated photographs, publicity and materials.','Describe impact on the HOSA chapter as well as the community.'],sections:[
['Outputs and outcomes',[
'**Outputs** count what you did: **hours contributed**, number of **events**, **materials distributed**, **people reached** and **people trained**. **Outcomes** describe what changed: more residents **vaccinated**, families with a **written emergency plan**, participants who can **demonstrate a skill** such as hands-only CPR, or **improved knowledge** on a short quiz. Outcomes are more powerful, but both matter.',
'Choose metrics **before** the activity with your MRC partner. For a preparedness fair, outputs might be families attending and kits distributed; outcomes might be the number who completed a family plan on site. For a drill, outputs might be student volunteers and hours; outcomes might be the number of responders who practiced triage.',
'Always describe **how** you measured each number—a sign-in sheet, an MRC count, a brief anonymous survey—so judges trust your data.',
sort('Output or outcome?',['Output','Outcome'],[['300 flyers distributed',0],['85 families completed an emergency plan',1],['48 volunteer hours',0],['60 participants demonstrated hands-only CPR',1],['4 events held',0],['Quiz scores improved from 50% to 85%',1]])]],
['Tracking hours and people impacted',[
'**Volunteer hours** are the most basic metric and appear on the Verification Form as **total hours volunteered with the MRC** by all team members. Record hours at each event with sign-in and sign-out times, include planning meetings and training if your MRC partner counts them, and keep a running spreadsheet.',
'**People impacted** may be people served (clinic clients), reached (flyer recipients), trained (CPR participants) or supported (shelter residents). Count carefully and avoid double counting. If the MRC provides totals for an event, cite them and explain your chapter’s share of the work.',
'Calculate totals correctly: if **8 students** each volunteer **6 hours**, that is **48 hours**. Show the math in your notes so numbers are consistent across the portfolio and presentation.',
chk('Five team members each volunteer 12 hours at an MRC shelter exercise. How many total hours should be recorded?',['60 hours','17 hours','12 hours','5 hours'],'5 × 12 = 60.')]],
['Photographs, publicity and materials',[
'The portfolio’s summary section may include **publicity** about the partnership—with the **publicity date** and a copy of the article, radio or TV spot or script—plus **programs**, **photographs** and other **dated verification** of activities, and copies of items you developed, such as pamphlets or brochures.',
'Take photographs at **every** event (with permission), showing students working with MRC volunteers. Caption each with the **date**, **location** and **activity**. Choose photos that show **action and partnership**, not just posed groups.',
'Keep a digital folder for each activity containing the plan, approvals, sign-in sheets, photos, materials and the after-action review. This makes writing the portfolio much easier in the spring.',
order('Documentation folder for each activity',['Activity plan and SMART objectives','MRC approvals','Sign-in sheets and hours','Dated photographs with captions','Materials created','Metrics and results','After-action review'])]],
['Impact on the community',[
'Judges score **impact on the local community**, with the highest rating for **four or more examples** showing a high level of impact and positive change. Examples might include residents vaccinated, families who completed emergency plans, community members trained in Stop the Bleed or CPR, vulnerable residents served or improved MRC response capacity.',
'Make impact concrete by connecting numbers to the **community need**: “Our county had a low flu vaccination rate among seniors; our outreach helped the MRC vaccinate 120 seniors at three senior centers.” Quotes from your MRC partner or community members (with permission) add credibility.',
'Be honest. If an outcome was not measured, describe the output clearly instead of guessing at results.',
"Present numbers in a simple table in the portfolio—activity, date, students, hours and people impacted—with a total row. Judges can see the scope of the partnership at a glance, and the totals should match the impact data on the Verification Form.",
sort('Strong or weak impact statement?',['Strong','Weak'],[['“Helped the MRC vaccinate 120 seniors at three centers in a county with low senior flu coverage”',0],['“We helped a lot”',1],['“Trained 45 coaches in Stop the Bleed; 45 demonstrated tourniquet use”',0],['“It was a great event”',1],['“60 families completed written emergency plans”',0]])]],
['Impact on the HOSA chapter',[
'Judges also score **impact on the HOSA chapter**, again with the top rating for **four or more examples**. Impact on the chapter includes **new skills** (CPR, Stop the Bleed, psychological first aid, moulage), **certifications**, **leadership growth**, **career exploration** through mentoring and shadowing, **increased membership or engagement**, new **community connections** and members who joined the MRC or chose health careers.',
'Measure chapter impact, too: number of members trained, hours of mentoring, members who earned certifications, a short survey of members’ confidence before and after the partnership and stories of members whose career plans were shaped by the experience.',
'Strong teams show that the partnership changed **both** the community and the chapter, creating a mutually beneficial relationship that can continue.',
match('Chapter impact and evidence',[['New skills','12 members earned Stop the Bleed training'],['Career exploration','5 members completed 30 hours of MRC mentoring'],['Leadership','Each member led at least one activity'],['Engagement','Chapter membership grew from 20 to 32'],['Confidence','Preparedness confidence survey rose from 2.5 to 4.3 out of 5'],['Continued service','Three graduates joined the MRC']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Writing up results',{a:{text:'After a preparedness fair, your notes say “lots of families came.” (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you have recorded?',choices:[['Sign-in counts of families, students and hours, and how many completed a plan','b','Correct.',true],['Nothing more—judges trust estimates','b','Specific metrics are required.',false]]},b:{text:'Eight students volunteered 5 hours each, and 64 of 150 families completed plans. How do you report this?',choices:[['40 student hours; 150 families reached; 64 (43%) completed written plans','c','Correct.',true],['Hundreds of hours and thousands helped','c','Report accurate numbers.',false]]},c:{text:'You add dated photos and a quote from the MRC unit leader to the activity folder.',end:true}})]],
["Collecting feedback and stories",["Numbers show scale; **stories** show meaning. With permission, collect short **quotes** from participants, MRC volunteers and your unit leader—for example, a senior who said the outage workshop helped her plan for her oxygen machine. Ask for written permission to use names, or use first names or roles only.","Short **feedback forms** (three questions, anonymous) can measure knowledge or confidence and gather suggestions. Ask the MRC to share any data they collect at joint events, such as total vaccinations given.","Combine both in the portfolio and presentation: “The MRC vaccinated 340 residents at our three clinics. One volunteer nurse told us our registration team cut wait times in half.”",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Collecting a participant quote","steps":["Ask permission to share their words","Ask one open question","Write the quote exactly","Record the date and activity","Confirm how they want to be identified","Store the permission with the quote"]}]]
],takeaways:['Outputs count activity (hours, events, materials, people reached or trained); outcomes show change (vaccinations, completed plans, demonstrated skills, improved knowledge); choose metrics with the MRC before each activity and explain how you measured them.','Track total hours volunteered with sign-in sheets for the Verification Form; count people impacted carefully; keep dated, captioned photos, publicity with dates, programs and materials in an activity folder.','Judges want four or more examples of impact on the community and four or more on the HOSA chapter (skills, certifications, leadership, careers, engagement); connect numbers to the community need and report honestly.'],terms:[['Output','A count of what was done, such as hours or materials distributed.'],['Outcome','A change that resulted from the activity.'],['Impact data','Summary numbers such as total hours volunteered with the MRC.']],mcqs:[q('Which is an outcome?','85 families completed an emergency plan','300 flyers distributed','4 events held','48 volunteer hours','Shows change.'),q('Which is an output?','Volunteer hours contributed','Improved quiz scores','Residents who changed behavior','Demonstrated CPR skill','Counts activity.'),q('Six students each volunteer 8 hours. Total hours?','48','14','8','6','6 × 8.'),q('What must accompany publicity in the portfolio?','The publicity date and a copy of the item','Nothing','Only a link','A reporter’s phone number','Dated verification.'),q('How many examples earn the highest score for community impact?','Four or more','One','Two','Ten','Strong evidence.'),q('Which shows impact on the HOSA chapter?','Members earned Stop the Bleed training','The MRC bought new tents','A storm occurred','A judge was impressed','Chapter growth.'),q('Photos in the portfolio should be:','Dated and captioned, taken with permission','Undated selfies','Of clients without consent','Stock images','Verification.'),q('Why describe how each number was measured?','So judges trust the data','To make the portfolio longer','It is not useful','To hide errors','Credibility.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would measure and document the impact of its MRC partnership on both the community and the HOSA chapter.',rubric:[['Metrics',['outputs','outcomes','hours','people','reached','trained','completed']],['Tracking',['sign-in','spreadsheet','verification form','total hours','measured','survey']],['Documentation',['photographs','dated','captions','publicity','materials','folder','permission']],['Impact',['community','chapter','four','examples','skills','careers','need','honest']]],model:'Before each activity, we would agree with our MRC unit leader on output and outcome metrics. Outputs would include volunteer hours, events held, materials distributed and people reached or trained; outcomes would include residents vaccinated, families completing emergency plans and participants who could demonstrate hands-only CPR or tourniquet use. We would record hours with sign-in and sign-out sheets at every event, keep a running spreadsheet whose total feeds the Verification Form’s impact data and count people impacted carefully using our sign-ins, MRC counts or short anonymous surveys, noting how each number was measured. For every activity we would keep a folder with the plan, MRC approvals, sign-in sheets, dated and captioned photos taken with permission, materials we created, publicity with its date and the after-action review. To show community impact, we would connect our numbers to the need—for example, helping the MRC vaccinate 120 seniors in a county with low senior flu coverage and training 45 coaches in Stop the Bleed—and aim for at least four strong examples. To show chapter impact, we would track members who earned training or certifications, mentoring hours, leadership roles, membership growth, a before-and-after confidence survey and members who joined the MRC or chose health careers. We would report every number honestly, even when results were smaller than hoped.'},sources:[RULES,MRC,STOP_BLEED]};

export const MR8={title:'Building the portfolio',hook:'Your portfolio is the judges’ only window into a year of partnership work. They will score it before they ever meet you, using a checklist that rewards completeness, clear descriptions and exact formatting. A portfolio that tells a clear story—need, partnership, action, impact—within 16 pages sets up a strong Round Two.',objectives:['Organize the portfolio in the required order within 16 pages.','Apply the narrative formatting rules exactly.','Write a strong partnership description and activity summaries.','Avoid errors that cost points on the rating sheet.'],sections:[
['Required contents and order',[
'The portfolio is limited to **16 numbered single-sided pages** and must contain, **in order**: **(a)** a **title page** with the event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association, **centered** (creative design or pictures are allowed but not scored); **(b)** the signed **HOSA/MRC Partnership Verification Form**; **(c)** the **Partnership Logistics Document**, signed at the beginning and after review, with the leader’s full mailing address; **(d)** a **description of the HOSA/MRC partnership** with the level, quality and quantity of interactions; and **(e)** a **summary section** of partnership activities.',
'Pages beyond **16** are **not judged**, so plan your space. A typical layout: title (1), Verification Form (1–2), Logistics Document (2), partnership description (2–3) and activities with supporting photos and materials (8–10).',
'Upload the portfolio as **one combined PDF** by **May 15**. If it is not a single PDF, judges will not score it, and any links or documents they cannot access earn no points.',
order('Portfolio order',['Title page (centered)','Partnership Verification Form','Partnership Logistics Document','Description of the HOSA/MRC partnership','Summary section of partnership activities'])]],
['Formatting the narrative pages',[
'All **narrative pages** must be **typed**, **one-sided**, in **12-point Arial**, **double-spaced**, in **English**, with **1-inch margins** on **8½ × 11-inch** paper, with a **running header**: **last name and event name at the top left** and the **page number at the top right**, not counting the title page. Formatting is scored as all-or-nothing, so every narrative page must comply.',
'Set up a template document with these settings before anyone writes, so all team members’ sections match. Check headers carefully—teams often forget the page number or put the header on the title page.',
'Neatness is also scored: **no errors** earns full points, while **1–2 errors** already lowers the score. Have someone outside the team proofread every page for spelling, grammar and consistent formatting.',
flip('Narrative formatting rules',[['Font','12-point Arial'],['Spacing','Double-spaced'],['Margins','1 inch on 8½ × 11 paper'],['Sides','One-sided'],['Header left','Last name and event name'],['Header right','Page number (title page not counted)']])]],
['Describing the partnership',[
'The **description of the HOSA/MRC partnership** is scored twice: for **including** the **level**, **quality** and **quantity** of interactions (5 points) and for the **strength and articulation** of the description (10 points). **Level** describes how deeply you were involved (members, partners, roles); **quality** describes how meaningful and collaborative the interactions were; **quantity** gives numbers—meetings, events, hours and people impacted.',
'Tell the story: how the partnership began, the community need you and the MRC identified, how you communicated, what you accomplished together and how it grew. Use specific examples and data, and connect your work to the **MRC mission**.',
'Write in clear, active sentences. For example: “Over nine months, our 6 members met monthly with the County MRC coordinator, completed MRC orientation and contributed 214 hours across five activities that reached 1,150 residents.”',
match('Description element and example',[['Level','Members completed MRC orientation and served as volunteers'],['Quality','Co-planned each activity with the MRC coordinator'],['Quantity','214 hours, 9 meetings, 1,150 residents reached'],['Need','Low preparedness among non-English-speaking families'],['Mission link','Built community resilience and public health'],['Growth','The MRC invited us to plan next year’s drill']])]],
['Writing activity summaries',[
'Each activity in the summary section includes three items: **(1)** the **activity name and description**; **(2)** the **impact category**; and **(3)** the **HOSA/MRC partnership interaction description**—how the chapter worked with the MRC in **preparation and planning**, the **roles and responsibilities** during the activity and the **importance and impact**, including **key metrics** such as hours and people impacted. All three must be present for full points.',
'Activity quality is scored separately (10 points) on **quality, scope and value**, so lead with your strongest activities. Add dated photos, programs and materials you developed next to each summary, and keep descriptions concise so they fit the page limit.',
'Use a consistent format for every activity—bold headings for “Activity,” “Impact Category” and “HOSA/MRC Partnership Interaction”—so judges can find each required item instantly.',
order('Activity summary format',['Activity name','Description of what happened','Impact category','How the team planned with the MRC','Roles during the activity','Importance and impact','Key metrics: hours and people impacted'])]],
['Rating-sheet checklist',[
'Round One is worth **60 points**: **title page** (5), **Verification Form** with four items (5), **Logistics Document** with all answers (5), **partnership description items** (5), **partnership description quality** (10), **activity quality** (10), **activity items included** (5), **neat and error-free** (5), **narrative formatting** (5) and **page limit** (5). Several items are **all-or-nothing**, so one missing element can cost all of that item’s points.',
'Before uploading, check each item against the rating sheet, confirm the MRC leader’s **final signature** after reviewing the portfolio and make sure the file is one PDF under 16 pages. Upload a few days before **May 15** and save the confirmation.',
'If you competed at a state conference using the HOSA Digital Upload System, you must **upload again** for ILC.',
sort('Earns the points or loses them?',['Earns the points','Loses points'],[['Title page centered with every required item',0],['17-page portfolio',1],['Narrative in 11-point Times New Roman',1],['Verification Form with both MRC signatures, address and total hours',0],['Activity missing its impact category',1],['Logistics Document answering every question',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Final review',{a:{text:'Your portfolio is 18 pages with large photos. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Trim to 16 pages by resizing photos and tightening text','b','Correct. Pages over 16 are not judged.',true],['Upload as is','b','You would lose the page-limit points and two pages would not be judged.',false]]},b:{text:'A teammate’s section uses single spacing to save room. What do you do?',choices:[['Change it to double spacing; formatting must match on every narrative page','c','Correct.',true],['Leave it; judges will not notice','c','Formatting is scored all-or-nothing.',false]]},c:{text:'You send the final version to the MRC leader for review and a final signature, then upload one PDF on May 10.',end:true}})]],
["A sample activity summary",["Use this invented example as a model for each portfolio entry. Keep the three required headings visible and include specific metrics.",{"t":"example","title":"Activity summary (invented example)","prompt":"Write a portfolio entry for a power-outage preparedness workshop.","steps":["Activity: “Ready When the Lights Go Out” workshops at three senior centers, teaching residents to write power-outage plans and assemble 72-hour kits.","Impact category: Serve a vulnerable population.","HOSA/MRC partnership interaction: The MRC coordinator identified the need after last winter’s outages, reviewed our booklet and assigned a nurse volunteer to answer questions about medical devices.","Roles: students led registration, taught the kit checklist and helped residents complete plans; the MRC nurse supervised and answered medical questions.","Importance and impact: 6 students contributed 36 hours; 92 residents attended; 75 completed written plans."],"answer":"All three required items appear, with specific roles and metrics (example invented for practice)."},"Place one dated photo and a sample page of the booklet next to the summary to verify the activity without using much space."]]
],takeaways:['Portfolio: ≤16 numbered single-sided pages in order—centered title page, Verification Form, Logistics Document, partnership description and activity summaries—uploaded as one PDF by May 15; pages over 16 are not judged.','Narrative pages: typed, one-sided, 12-pt Arial, double-spaced, English, 1-inch margins on 8½ × 11, running header with last name and event name top left and page number top right; proofread for zero errors.','Partnership description covers level, quality and quantity with a strong story; each activity has a name and description, impact category and partnership interaction description with metrics; check all 60 rating-sheet points and get the MRC leader’s final signature.'],terms:[['Portfolio','The pre-judged document that presents the partnership and its activities.'],['Running header','Text repeated at the top of each page with name, event and page number.'],['Summary section','The part of the portfolio describing each partnership activity.']],mcqs:[q('What is the maximum number of portfolio pages?','16','10','25','30','Pages beyond 16 are not judged.'),q('Which font is required for narrative pages?','12-point Arial','10-point Times','14-point Calibri','Any font','Double-spaced.'),q('Where does the page number go?','Top right','Bottom center','Top left','Nowhere','Title page not counted.'),q('Which comes first in the portfolio?','Title page','Activity summaries','Logistics Document','Photos','Then the Verification Form.'),q('The partnership description must include:','Level, quality and quantity of interactions','Only photos','Only the MRC mission','The judges’ names','Three items.'),q('How many points is Round One worth?','60','100','70','130','Pre-judged.'),q('Which three items does each activity summary need?','Name and description, impact category, partnership interaction description','Title, author, date','Photos, music, video','Budget, cost, receipts','All three for full points.'),q('How many grammar or neatness errors still earn full points?','None','1–2','3–4','5 or more','Error-free.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would organize, write and format the MRC Partnership portfolio to earn full points on the Round One rating sheet.',rubric:[['Order and limits',['title page','verification form','logistics','description','summary','16 pages','one pdf','may 15']],['Formatting',['12','arial','double','1-inch','one-sided','header','last name','page number']],['Content',['level','quality','quantity','activity','impact category','interaction','metrics','hours']],['Quality checks',['proofread','error-free','rating sheet','signature','review','upload early']]],model:'We would build the portfolio in the required order within 16 numbered single-sided pages: a centered title page with the event name, team member names, HOSA division, chapter number, school name and chartered association; the Partnership Verification Form with the MRC unit’s full address, both of the leader’s signatures and our total volunteer hours; the Partnership Logistics Document answering how, how often and who maintains contact and how we supported MRC reporting, with the leader’s mailing address and signatures; a description of the partnership; and the summary section of activities. Before writing, we would create a template with 12-point Arial, double spacing, one-inch margins on letter-size paper and a running header with our last names and the event name on the top left and the page number on the top right, excluding the title page, so every narrative page matches. Our partnership description would cover the level of involvement, the quality of collaboration and the quantity of meetings, hours and people impacted, telling the story from the need we identified with the MRC to the results. Each activity summary would include the activity name and description, its impact category and a partnership interaction description of planning, roles and impact with key metrics, supported by dated photos. Finally, we would check every rating-sheet item, have someone outside the team proofread for zero errors, send the portfolio to our MRC leader for review and final signature and upload one PDF several days before May 15.'},sources:[RULES,UPLOADS,MRC]};

export const MR9={title:'The five-minute presentation',hook:'In Round Two, judges finally meet the people behind the portfolio. You have five minutes, no slides and no props—just your team, your voices and your story. The rating sheet rewards cooperative work with the MRC, understanding of the mission, impact on the community and chapter and creativity. A focused story about a real need and real results is what judges remember.',objectives:['List the required presentation content and the Round Two scoring.','Build a five-minute outline that tells a clear story.','Provide four or more examples for mission, community impact and chapter impact.','Involve every team member and use notecards effectively.'],sections:[
['What the presentation must include',[
'The presentation must include **(a)** a brief description of the **activities used to promote the partnership** and **(b)** the **accomplishment of goals and objectives** and the **impact** of the partnership and activities. The guidelines remind you that **quality over quantity** matters: showcase how your chapter worked with the MRC to identify and address a **specific community need**, then highlight the **impact and results**.',
'Round Two is worth **70 points**. **Content (50)**: cooperative work with the MRC to reach goals (10), description and understanding of the MRC mission (10), impact on the local community (10), impact on the HOSA chapter (10) and imagination and creativity of activities (10). **Delivery (20)**: voice, stage presence, diction and team participation (5 each).',
'Only **notecards** (paper, or electronic on a device not shown to judges) are allowed. **No other materials, props or visual aids**—not even the portfolio—may be shown. **All team members must take an active role.**',
match('Round Two criterion and points',[['Cooperative work with MRC','10'],['Understanding of MRC mission','10'],['Impact on the local community','10'],['Impact on the HOSA chapter','10'],['Imagination and creativity','10'],['Each delivery item','5']])]],
['A five-minute story',[
'Structure the presentation as a story: **(1) The need** (0:00–0:45)—what problem the community faced and how you and the MRC identified it. **(2) The partnership** (0:45–1:30)—how you connected with the MRC, your roles and how you worked together. **(3) The activities** (1:30–3:00)—your two or three strongest activities, with goals, creativity and results. **(4) The impact** (3:00–4:15)—community and chapter impact with numbers. **(5) The future** (4:15–5:00)—what comes next and a memorable close.',
'The timekeeper announces **one minute remaining** and stops you at **five minutes**. Rehearse to finish at about **4:45**. If you run long, cut detail from the activity section rather than from impact.',
'Open with a **hook**—a short scene from an activity, a striking number or a quote from your MRC partner—to draw judges in immediately.',
"Choose one memorable theme for the whole presentation, such as “ready together,” and return to it at the end. A clear theme helps judges remember your team among many others.",
order('Five-minute story',['Hook and the community need','How the partnership worked','Two or three strongest activities','Impact on the community and the chapter','Next steps and a memorable close'])]],
['Four or more examples',[
'Three content criteria—**MRC mission**, **community impact** and **chapter impact**—give the top score for **four or more examples**. Plan these examples deliberately and say them clearly. For the **mission**, connect activities to strengthening public health, emergency response capability and community resilience: “By playing victims in the county drill, we helped 40 responders practice triage—building response capability.”',
'For **community impact**, use specific results: residents vaccinated, families with plans, people trained. For **chapter impact**, describe new skills, certifications, leadership growth, career exploration and membership growth. Keep a simple tally on your notecards so you know you hit four of each.',
'Spread examples across speakers so every member contributes evidence, not just introductions.',
chk('How many examples of community impact earn the highest score?',['Four or more','One','Two','None'],'Strong evidence requires four or more examples.')]],
['Cooperative work and creativity',[
'**Cooperative work with the MRC** (10 points) is about how well you **collaborated to reach the activities’ goals**. Show joint planning, shared roles, regular communication and how the MRC relied on your chapter. Mention goals you set together and whether you **exceeded** them.',
'**Imagination and creativity** (10 points) reward fresh ideas: a preparedness game for children, bilingual heat-safety videos approved by the MRC, a “Stop the Bleed” day for coaches or a moulage workshop. Explain what made your approach creative and why it worked for your audience.',
'Avoid listing activities without explanation. For each one, briefly answer: Why this activity? What did we do with the MRC? What changed?',
sort('Shows cooperation with the MRC?',['Strong evidence','Weak evidence'],[['“We co-planned each event with the MRC coordinator and met our 300-family goal”',0],['“We did some things”',1],['“The MRC asked us to lead registration at its regional drill”',0],['“We worked alone and told the MRC afterward”',1],['“We held monthly planning calls with the unit leader”',0]])]],
['Notes, roles and rules',[
'Use **index cards** with key words, numbers and transitions—not full scripts. Electronic notecards are allowed only if the device is **not shown** to judges. Do not bring posters, slides, the portfolio or props to show.',
'Divide the presentation so **every member speaks** with substance. For a team of six, each person might take one section and one impact example. Practice **transitions** (“Now Jordan will share what changed in our community”) and decide who will handle any unexpected moment, like a lost line.',
'Rehearse with a timer at least five times and once in front of an audience, ideally including your MRC partner, who can confirm that your descriptions are accurate.',
sort('Allowed in the MRC Partnership presentation?',['Allowed','Not allowed'],[['Paper index cards',0],['A PowerPoint',1],['Holding up the portfolio for judges',1],['Electronic notes on a device not shown to judges',0],['A poster of activity photos',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Rehearsal feedback',{a:{text:'Your rehearsal lists eight activities quickly but gives no results. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you change?',choices:[['Focus on two or three strongest activities with goals and measured impact','b','Correct. Quality over quantity.',true],['Add more activities','b','Judges value impact.',false]]},b:{text:'Your notes show only two examples of chapter impact. What do you add?',choices:[['Two more, such as certifications earned and membership growth','c','Correct. Aim for four or more.',true],['Nothing; two is enough','c','Four or more earns the top score.',false]]},c:{text:'Your next rehearsal tells a clear story, finishes at 4:45 and includes four examples each for mission, community and chapter impact.',end:true}})]],
["Opening, closing and handling nerves",["Open with a **hook** that puts judges in the moment: “At 6 a.m. on a Saturday, twelve HOSA members were setting up check-in tables before the county’s first flu clinic of the season.” Then state the community need in one sentence.","Close by connecting your work to the **MRC mission** and the future: “Together with our MRC unit, we strengthened public health, improved preparedness and built a partnership our chapter will continue next year.” End confidently and thank the judges.","Nerves are normal. Breathe slowly before entering, know your first line by heart and trust your teammates to help if someone forgets a line. Smile—enthusiasm is part of stage presence.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Strong or weak opening?","buckets":["Strong","Weak"],"items":[["A vivid moment from a real activity",0],["“Um, hi, so we did MRC”",1],["A striking number tied to the community need",0],["Reading the title page aloud",1],["A short quote from the MRC unit leader",0]]}]]
],takeaways:['Must include activities used to promote the partnership and the accomplishment of goals, objectives and impact; quality over quantity—focus on a specific community need addressed with the MRC.','Round Two is 70 points: cooperative work, MRC mission, community impact, chapter impact and creativity (10 each) plus voice, stage presence, diction and team participation (5 each).','Tell a five-minute story (need, partnership, activities, impact, future) finishing about 4:45; give four or more examples for mission, community impact and chapter impact; notecards only—no visual aids; every member speaks.'],terms:[['Hook','An opening that grabs the judges’ attention.'],['Cooperative work','Collaboration with the MRC to reach shared goals.'],['Quality over quantity','Emphasizing meaningful, impactful activities rather than many small ones.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is Round Two worth?','70','60','130','50','Content 50 + delivery 20.'),q('Which may be used during the presentation?','Index cards','The portfolio held up for judges','A PowerPoint','A poster','Notecards only.'),q('How many examples earn the top score for MRC mission?','Four or more','One','Two','Three only','Strong evidence.'),q('Which criterion rewards fresh ideas?','Imagination and creativity','Voice','Page limit','Title page','10 points.'),q('What must every team member do?','Take an active role in the presentation','Stay silent','Hold the props','Leave early','All members.'),q('When does the timekeeper give a warning?','At one minute remaining','At three minutes','Never','At 30 seconds after time','Then stops at five.'),q('Which statement best shows cooperative work?','“We co-planned each event with the MRC and met our goal”','“We did some things”','“We worked alone”','“We forgot to call the MRC”','Joint planning.'),q('What should a team cut first if running long?','Extra activity detail','The impact section','Everyone’s speaking parts','The community need','Protect impact.')],frq:{prompt:'Create a five-minute presentation plan for MRC Partnership that covers the required content and earns top scores for mission, community impact, chapter impact and cooperative work.',rubric:[['Structure',['hook','need','partnership','activities','impact','future','4:45','5 minutes']],['Mission and cooperation',['mission','public health','response','resilience','co-planned','goals','mrc']],['Impact examples',['community','chapter','four','examples','vaccinated','trained','skills','certifications']],['Rules and roles',['notecards','no visual aids','every member','transitions','rehearse']]],model:'Our five-member team will open with a hook from our MRC partner: “You were the reason 120 seniors got their flu shots this year.” From 0:00 to 0:45, Ava explains the need—our county’s low flu vaccination rate among seniors and poor power-outage preparedness—which we identified with the MRC coordinator. From 0:45 to 1:30, Ben describes the partnership: we chose Path B, met monthly with the coordinator and co-planned every activity with shared goals. From 1:30 to 3:00, Cara and Dev present our three strongest activities—outreach and registration for three senior flu clinics, power-outage preparedness workshops and serving as moulaged victims in the county drill—highlighting our creative large-print, bilingual planning booklet and the goals we exceeded. From 3:00 to 4:15, Eli and Ava share impact with four examples each: for the community, 120 seniors vaccinated, 85 completed outage plans, 40 responders trained and 1,150 residents reached; for the chapter, 12 members trained in Stop the Bleed, five completed 30 hours of mentoring, membership grew from 20 to 32 and two seniors plan nursing careers. Throughout, we connect activities to the MRC mission of strengthening public health, response capability and resilience. From 4:15 to 4:45, Ben describes next steps and closes. We use only index cards with key numbers, every member speaks with planned transitions and we rehearse to 4:45, once in front of our MRC coordinator.'},sources:[RULES,MRC]};

export const MR10={title:'Delivery, sustaining the partnership and careers',hook:'The partnership does not have to end on May 15. Many chapters keep working with their MRC units year after year, and some members go on to careers in public health, nursing and emergency management. This final lesson covers polished delivery, ways to sustain and grow the partnership and the careers this event can open.',objectives:['Apply the four delivery criteria in Round Two.','Plan how to sustain the partnership beyond the competition year.','Describe trainings that build members’ preparedness skills.','Explore careers in public health and emergency preparedness.'],sections:[
['Delivery that matches your content',[
'Delivery is worth **20 points**: **voice** (volume, varied rate, pauses), **stage presence** (poise, posture, eye contact, enthusiasm and purposeful gestures), **diction, pronunciation and grammar** (clear words, no fillers) and **team participation** (shared collaboration with each member carrying an equal part).',
'Speak with **pride and specifics**. Numbers land better when you pause before them: “Together, we contributed… 214 hours.” Make eye contact with each judge, stand still while speaking and keep your notecards low. Teammates who are not speaking should look at the speaker and react naturally.',
"Use your voice to show the human side of the work. Slow down when describing the people you served, and let your enthusiasm show when describing the partnership’s growth. Judges notice when a team genuinely cares about its community.",
'Record a rehearsal to catch fillers (“um,” “like”) and rushing. Practice pronouncing terms such as “moulage” (moo-LAHZH) and “Administration for Strategic Preparedness and Response.”',
sort('Strong or weak delivery?',['Strong','Weak'],[['Pausing before a key number',0],['Reading every word from a card',1],['Eye contact with each judge',0],['Saying “um” between sentences',1],['Each member speaking an equal share',0],['One member answering everything',1]])]],
['Sustaining the partnership',[
'Strong partnerships continue. Before the year ends, meet with your unit leader to **review** what worked, **thank** volunteers and discuss **next year’s** needs and activities. Update the Logistics Document for the new year, and introduce **younger members** to the MRC so the relationship continues after seniors graduate.',
'Create a **partnership handbook** for your chapter with contacts, procedures, approval steps, activity templates, photo consent forms and lessons learned. Share your results with your school, local media (with MRC approval) and the MRC unit’s stakeholders to build support.',
'Sustained partnerships often grow into **bigger roles**—leading a youth preparedness program, helping plan an annual drill or creating a school-based MRC youth group where allowed.',
order('Sustaining the partnership',['Hold an end-of-year review with the MRC','Thank volunteers and partners','Discuss next year’s needs','Introduce younger members','Write a partnership handbook','Share results with the community'])]],
['Building members’ preparedness skills',[
'Partnerships are a chance for members to gain **training**. Common options include **CPR and AED**, **Stop the Bleed**, **first aid**, **psychological first aid (PFA)**—which teaches how to support people after disasters by listening, providing comfort and connecting them to help—and FEMA’s free online **Independent Study** courses such as introductions to the **Incident Command System** and the **National Incident Management System**.',
'MRC units may offer **orientation**, **shelter operations**, **POD** and **moulage** training. Track trainings completed by members; they are strong evidence of **impact on the HOSA chapter** and prepare members for future volunteering.',
"Ask your MRC partner whether members can attend unit trainings alongside adult volunteers. Learning with experienced responders shows students how preparedness works in practice and builds relationships that can lead to mentoring, recommendations and future volunteer roles.",
'Skills learned through the partnership also help members in other HOSA events, such as CERT Skills, CPR/First Aid and Emergency Medical Technician.',
match('Training and what it teaches',[['CPR and AED','Responding to cardiac arrest'],['Stop the Bleed','Controlling severe bleeding'],['Psychological first aid','Supporting people after disasters'],['ICS introduction','Organizing incident operations'],['Shelter operations','Running a safe shelter'],['Moulage','Creating realistic injuries for drills']])]],
['Careers in public health and emergency preparedness',[
'Partnership experience connects to many careers. **Public health** careers include epidemiologist, health educator, environmental health specialist and public health nurse. **Emergency preparedness** careers include **emergency management director**, preparedness coordinator, hospital emergency manager and disaster recovery specialist. **Clinical** careers include nurse, physician, physician assistant, pharmacist, paramedic and EMT—many of whom volunteer with the MRC.',
'The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics describes **emergency management directors** as professionals who prepare plans and procedures for responding to natural disasters and other emergencies and help lead the response, often working for local and state governments, hospitals and universities. Most positions require a bachelor’s degree plus experience.',
'Ask MRC volunteers about their careers during mentoring or shadowing. Their advice—and the connections you build—can shape your education and career path.',
match('Career and main focus',[['Emergency management director','Plans and leads disaster response'],['Epidemiologist','Investigates disease patterns'],['Public health nurse','Community health and vaccination'],['Health educator','Teaches healthy behaviors'],['Paramedic','Advanced prehospital care'],['Hospital emergency manager','Hospital disaster plans']])]],
['Volunteering as a lifelong commitment',[
'Volunteering builds **community**, **skills** and **purpose**. Many MRC volunteers serve for years, responding to hurricanes, outbreaks and community events. As adults—and sometimes earlier, depending on unit policies—HOSA members can join an MRC unit, CERT team or the American Red Cross.',
'Volunteering also supports **personal well-being**: helping others builds connection and meaning. At the same time, responders should practice **self-care**—rest, healthy routines and talking about stressful experiences—because disaster work can be emotionally demanding.',
'The partnership is a model of how future health professionals can serve: prepared, collaborative and focused on the whole community.',
chk('Why should volunteers practice self-care?',['Disaster and emergency work can be emotionally demanding','It is not needed','To avoid volunteering','To impress judges'],'Rest and support help volunteers serve safely and sustainably.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Graduating seniors',{a:{text:'Four of your five team members graduate this year. (Scenario invented for practice.) How do you keep the MRC partnership going?',choices:[['Introduce younger members to the MRC and leave a partnership handbook','b','Correct.',true],['Let it end when you graduate','b','Plan for sustainability.',false]]},b:{text:'The unit leader asks whether your chapter could help plan next year’s regional drill. How do you respond?',choices:[['Agree, discuss roles and update the Logistics Document for next year','c','Correct.',true],['Decline because the competition is over','c','Partnerships can continue beyond the event.',false]]},c:{text:'At your end-of-year meeting, you thank the volunteers and set next year’s first planning date.',end:true}})]],
["Reflecting on the year",["Before your final presentation, reflect as a team. What community need did you address, and how did the partnership change your community? What did each member learn about public health and emergency preparedness? Which moments were most meaningful? What would you do differently?","Write down **four or more** concrete examples of how the partnership changed your chapter—new skills, certifications, leadership, career plans or membership growth—and practice describing them in one sentence each. These reflections strengthen both your presentation and your future applications for scholarships, college or jobs.","Finally, thank everyone who made the partnership possible: your MRC unit leader and volunteers, your advisor, your school and the community members you served.",{"t":"w","kind":"order","title":"Team reflection","steps":["Name the community need and your response","List impact on the community","List impact on the chapter","Share personal lessons","Identify improvements for next year","Thank partners and supporters"]}]]
],takeaways:['Delivery (20 points): voice, stage presence, diction and team participation; pause before key numbers, make eye contact, avoid fillers and have every member carry an equal share.','Sustain the partnership with an end-of-year review, thank-yous, next-year planning, younger members, a partnership handbook and shared results; partnerships can grow into larger roles.','Members can earn CPR/AED, Stop the Bleed, psychological first aid and FEMA ICS/NIMS training; the partnership opens careers in public health, emergency management and clinical care; volunteering is lifelong, with self-care.'],terms:[['Psychological first aid','An approach to supporting people in the hours and days after a disaster.'],['Emergency management director','A professional who plans for and leads response to emergencies.'],['Partnership handbook','A chapter guide with contacts, procedures and lessons for continuing the partnership.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is delivery worth in Round Two?','20','5','50','70','Four criteria × 5.'),q('Which shows strong delivery?','Pausing before a key number','Reading every word','Many fillers','Speaking only to one judge','Emphasis.'),q('Which helps sustain the partnership after seniors graduate?','Introducing younger members to the MRC','Deleting contacts','Ending all communication','Keeping it secret','Continuity.'),q('Psychological first aid teaches how to:','Support people after disasters','Perform surgery','Prescribe medication','Write portfolios','Comfort and connect.'),q('Which FEMA course topic organizes incident operations?','Incident Command System','Medical billing','Nutrition','Accounting','ICS.'),q('An emergency management director:','Plans and leads response to emergencies','Only treats patients','Only writes news stories','Designs clothing','BLS description.'),q('Why practice self-care as a volunteer?','Emergency work can be emotionally demanding','It is not important','To avoid helping','It is required by judges','Sustainable service.'),q('What should a partnership handbook include?','Contacts, procedures and lessons learned','Only photos','Personal opinions','Nothing','For future members.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your chapter would sustain its MRC partnership after the competition and how the partnership can benefit members’ skills and careers.',rubric:[['Sustaining',['review','thank','next year','younger members','handbook','logistics','share results']],['Growth',['larger roles','plan','drill','youth','continue']],['Skills',['cpr','stop the bleed','psychological first aid','ics','training','certifications']],['Careers and service',['public health','emergency management','nurse','epidemiologist','mentoring','volunteer','self-care']]],model:'To sustain our partnership, we would hold an end-of-year review with our MRC unit leader to discuss what worked, what we would change and the unit’s needs for next year, and we would thank the volunteers who mentored and supervised us. Because most of our team is graduating, we would bring younger members to the meeting, introduce them to the coordinator and update the Partnership Logistics Document for the new year. We would also write a partnership handbook with contacts, approval steps, activity templates, photo consent forms and lessons from our after-action reviews, and share our results with the school and community with the MRC’s approval. Over time, the partnership could grow into larger roles, such as helping plan the county’s annual drill or leading a youth preparedness program. The partnership benefits members by building skills—CPR and AED, Stop the Bleed, psychological first aid and FEMA’s introductory Incident Command System courses—and by giving them mentoring from MRC nurses, paramedics and public health workers. Those experiences open paths to careers in public health, such as epidemiology and health education; emergency preparedness, such as emergency management director; and clinical care, such as nursing and paramedicine. Most importantly, members learn that volunteering is a lifelong way to serve their community, and that caring for their own well-being helps them serve sustainably.'},sources:[RULES,PFA,BLS_EM,MRC]};
